# Frontend

Frontend behavior is part of correctness. A compiling page is not a finished change.

Use semantic HTML and accessible names. Keyboard users can reach, operate, and leave every control. Manage focus when dialogs, navigation, and inline errors appear.

Design loading, empty, error, and partial-failure states as part of the feature. Streaming and long-running work need a visible status and a way to cancel or retry when the product allows it.

Keep client components small and push fetch boundaries to the server when Next.js already provides them. Do not render raw JSON as the interface.

When browser tools exist, verify desktop, tablet, and mobile for the changed flow, including overflow, focus, and error states. Say when that did not happen.
